course: biology
lesson: 299
title: The big biology quiz, part 2: plants, the body and microbes
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Part two of the big quiz looks at the living things we often forget: plants that make their own food, mushrooms that are not plants at all, the tiny germs and helpful microbes all around us, and the body you live in.
explain: Think of baking bread at home: you mix flour, water and a spoon of yeast, and an hour later the dough has puffed up. That is a living fungus at work, eating sugar and breathing out gas bubbles.
explain: The same few ideas answer many questions: plants make food from light, every living thing carries a recipe called DNA, and germs come in different kinds, so each needs the right weapon.
explain: Myths love this part of biology, like «antibiotics cure colds» or «the tongue has a map», so a good quiz player checks what really causes what.
fact: Plants need light, water, air and nutrients from the soil; they make their own food from sunlight, water and carbon dioxide and give out oxygen.
fact: Flowers make seeds after pollination by bees, wind or birds, and the fruit grows around the seeds to help spread them.
fact: Mushrooms are fungi, a kingdom of their own; fungi cannot make food from sunlight and are closer to animals than to plants, and many wild mushrooms are deadly, so never pick them to eat.
fact: An adult has 206 bones, while a baby has about 300 that join as it grows; the smallest bone is in the ear and the biggest is the thigh bone.
fact: The heart is a muscle about the size of your fist that pumps blood around the body about 100000 times a day, and blood carries oxygen and food to every cell.
fact: Germs are living things far too small to see without a microscope, such as bacteria, or viruses, that can make us ill; washing hands with soap removes many germs, and most bacteria are harmless or helpful, like those in your gut.
fact: Colds are caused by viruses, and antibiotics only work on bacteria, so rest, drinks and time help a cold, and using antibiotics wrongly helps resistant bacteria spread.
fact: A vaccine shows the body a harmless copy or part of a germ, so the body learns to fight it and is ready if the real germ comes; vaccines wiped out smallpox and have saved hundreds of millions of lives.
fact: Smell does a big part of tasting, so with a blocked nose food tastes flat; the tongue itself senses sweet, sour, salty, bitter and savoury all over, not in separate zones.
fact: In autumn trees stop making green chlorophyll, so yellow and orange colours that were hidden show through, some leaves make new red colours, and then the leaves fall to save water in winter.
fact: Yeast is a tiny living fungus that eats sugar and makes gas bubbles that make bread rise; it works best when warm, and the heat of the oven kills it.
fact: DNA is the recipe for a living thing, found in almost every cell; it is written with four chemical letters, and you get half from each parent, so your parents, brothers and sisters share more of your DNA than anyone else.
exercise: What do plants need to grow? | Light, water, air and nutrients from soil | Only soil, which they eat through their roots | Only water and darkness | Sugar and milk from the gardener every morning
exercise: What gas do plants give out when they make food? | Oxygen | Carbon dioxide | Smoke | Helium
exercise: Which part of a plant makes seeds? | The flower | The root | The green leaf | The bark of the stem
exercise: Is a mushroom a plant? | No, it is a fungus, a kingdom of its own | Yes, it is just a plant without any leaves or flowers | Yes, a kind of small tree | No, it is an animal
exercise: How many bones does an adult have? | 206 | 106 | 306 | 500
exercise: Where is the smallest bone in your body? | In the ear | In the little toe | In the nose | In the little finger
exercise: What does the heart do? | Pumps blood around the body | Cleans the air we breathe in | Makes all our thoughts and dreams | Digests our food
exercise: What are germs? | Tiny living things like bacteria, or viruses, that can make us ill | Specks of dust that float in the air and make us sneeze all day long | Small insects that live on our skin | Bits of dirt from the ground
exercise: Why do antibiotics not help a cold? | Colds are caused by viruses, and antibiotics work only on bacteria | Colds are far too strong for any medicine in the world to touch | Antibiotics work only in summer | Antibiotics only help grown-ups
exercise: How does a vaccine work? | It shows the body a harmless copy or part of a germ so it learns to fight it | It kills every single germ in the body at once, so you can never get ill again | It makes the blood too cold for germs | It coats the skin so germs slide off
exercise: Which disease was wiped out by vaccines? | Smallpox | The common cold | Chickenpox | Flu
exercise: Which sense helps the most with tasting food? | Smell | Hearing | Sight | Touch
exercise: Why do leaves turn yellow and orange in autumn? | The tree stops making green chlorophyll, so hidden colours show | The leaves are painted yellow and orange by the first frosts of the year | The leaves rust in the rain | The sun burns them orange
exercise: What does yeast do in bread dough? | Eats sugar and makes gas bubbles that make it rise | Melts the flour into a thick sweet syrup | Makes the dough hard and flat so it keeps | Adds a sweet taste only
exercise: What is DNA? | The recipe for a living thing, in almost every cell | A kind of germ that lives in the blood and makes us ill | A vitamin found in fruit | The name for a muscle in the leg
exercise: How much of your DNA comes from each parent? | Half from each | All from your mum | All from your dad | A quarter from each
test: «put my bean plant in the cupboard so it's safe, it'll be fine yeah» What will happen? | It will grow pale and weak, because it needs light to make food | It will grow faster in the dark, because plants like to rest | Nothing, plants only need water | It will turn into a mushroom
test: «found mushrooms in the park, they look just like the shop ones, we're having them for tea» What is right? | No, never eat wild mushrooms; many are deadly | Yes, if they look like shop ones they are safe | Yes, if you cook them long enough | Yes, but only the white ones
test: A baby has about 300 bones and an adult 206. Where do the missing bones go? | Many of the baby's bones join together as it grows | The body throws them out | They slowly melt away in the blood | Babies lose them when their milk teeth fall out, one by one
test: «got a cold, gimme those antibiotics from last year» What is the best answer? | No, antibiotics do not work on cold viruses, and old leftovers must not be taken | Yes, any antibiotic cures a cold, however old it is | Yes, but take double | Yes, if you mix them with orange juice
test: Count them: a class of 10 children each has a heart and 2 lungs. How many hearts and lungs in the room? | 10 hearts and 20 lungs | 20 hearts and 20 lungs | 10 hearts and 10 lungs | 10 hearts and 40 lungs
test: In a cartoon the germs are little green monsters with angry faces. What is the truth? | Germs are far too small to see, and most bacteria are harmless or helpful | Germs really are green and have faces, but you need glasses to see them | All bacteria are dangerous monsters | Germs are as big as ants
test: «my food tastes of nothing with this cold, my tongue's broken» What is really going on? | The blocked nose stops smell, which does a big part of tasting | The cold virus has killed the tongue and it will never taste again | Taste buds fall out during a cold | Food really loses its flavour in winter
test: «tongue map: sweet at the tip, bitter at the back, learned it at school» What is right? | Wrong; all five tastes are sensed all over the tongue | Right, each taste has its own zone on the tongue, like a map | Right, but only for children | Wrong, the tongue tastes only salt
test: Pine trees keep their needles all winter while oak trees drop their leaves. Why do oaks drop theirs? | To save water through the winter when the ground is frozen | Because the leaves get bored | To keep the birds warm | Because the wind blows them off by accident every year in the autumn storms
test: What would happen to the yeast in bread dough put into a hot oven? | The heat would kill it, so the bread stops rising | It would grow even faster for hours | It would turn into flour | It would fly out of the bread
test: «my mate says a vaccine gives you the actual disease, that's how it works» What is the best correction? | It shows the body a harmless copy or part of the germ, so it learns to fight it | Yes, it is the full disease in a tiny bottle, just a bit weaker | No, vaccines just make you sleepy | No, vaccines kill all your blood cells
test: Who shares the most DNA with you? | Your parents, brothers and sisters | Your best friend from school, if you are alike | Your pet dog | Your neighbours
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the classification of mushrooms. | Mushrooms are fungi, which constitute a kingdom distinct from plants | Mushrooms are plants devoid of leaves, flowers and chlorophyll | Mushrooms are animals of a sedentary character | Mushrooms are minerals formed in the soil
exam: Identify the principal reason for which antibiotics are ineffective in the treatment of the common cold. | The common cold is caused by viruses, upon which antibiotics have no effect | The common cold is caused by bacteria resistant to all medicines | Antibiotics are effective solely in persons of advanced age | Antibiotics act exclusively upon fungi
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the mode of action of a vaccine? | It presents to the body a harmless copy or part of a pathogen, whereby immunity is acquired | It destroys forthwith every micro-organism within the body | It lowers the temperature of the blood below that tolerated by pathogens, which thereupon perish | It forms a barrier upon the skin impermeable to pathogens
exam: State the number of bones ordinarily present in the skeleton of an adult human being. | Two hundred and six | One hundred and six | Three hundred and six | Five hundred
